Z||000012||Nashco||05-15-2001||03:10 AM||||First of all, it's obvious you have some problems.  Everybody's pointing at mechanical things, but perhaps that's not the problem.  If we put the VW driver in your car, and you in the VW, would the VW still win?  I think this is a matter of knowing how to "race" your car, rather than drive it smoothly.  I also owned a stock 1500cc VW, then 2 barreled 1600cc with some minor hopups..and no, there's no way you lost to it.  I never won a race with that car...but the 30" tires couldn't have helped.  Maybe that's the problem...you weren't racing in the sand dunes were you?  In that case, my VW would have won <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||05-15-2001||08:15 AM||||I think your problem are the 3.4 injectors with the "stock" PROM in ECM. Other member here was running rich with that setup. If you are running to rich your car performance will suck. But still... beat by VW???||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||09:53 AM||||WHOA WHOA WHOA!!!<P>you have a 3.4 L engine from some donor car<P>and you are still using the ECM from the 2.8L V6 !?!?<P>that cant be right! That cant possibly be right! Thats gotta be your problem - how can the fuel injection system and control from one engine work on another one?<P>I seem to remember reading somewhere that even between Fiero 2.8 V6's you cant swap the ECM from one car to another.<P>  And I dont think you are running rich (if you were the car would be blowing soot but would most likely run pretty strong at WFO - most likely the car is running way lean, and that why you are not getting 160 - 200HP out of it.<P>  Think about this for a second. The ECM tells the fuel injectors how much fuel to squirt into the cylinders. If its designed for a 2.8L engine, and you stick it on a 3.4 L engine, how is it suppose to know the clyinder volume is 24% larger than it was yesterday, and that it needs 24% more fuel?<P>  I know the primary feedback sensor for the ECM is the O2 sensor, and when it sees the exhaust is lean it tells the injectors to put more fuel out, but a 24% increase may be outside of its range, it may not be able to adjust itself that far.<P>  I have only been on this forum for a month now, but I have noticed that many times conflicting or simply outright wrong information is given out. I am not an expert on this subject by any means. But I think, most likely, you need the ECM from the donor car to go with the 3.4L engine you now have.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||05-15-2001||11:39 AM||||That's 88mph downhill with a 40mph tail wind.||||reg||10
Z||000016||N_Man||05-15-2001||12:54 PM||||Ken im gonna have to argue your last point.  The Fiero ECM is the correct choice because, the 3.4 is using all of the fieros intake and exaust equipment its basicly a big fiero motor and not a camero motor in any way.  Solution to this problem properly sized injectors OR a fuel pressure regulator.<BR>The ECM doesnt know the amount of fuel coming out of the injectors so the stock ECM with the appropriate fuel system mods will give the ideal performance.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||02:01 PM||||Okay, i admit I might be completely in the dark on this one, I didnt catch the fuel system mods you were talking about, different injectors, higher fuel pressure...<P>   the ECM is part of the fuel system, and it thinks it knows how much fuel is going into each cylinder cause it tells the injectors how long to fire (ok semantics)<P>  this does sound like the first thing to check out though, to make sure he has everything he needs on the engine to make whichever ECM is installed work properly.<P>  just out of couriosity, a stock V6 2.8L will go 0 to 60 in about 8.4 S (new new new everything). My car is stumbling a little, and I think I have a soft engine mount, so I timed mine over lunch, backing off the pedal a little, and i did 0 to 60 in 9.2 seconds. <P>  take a run in your 3.4 GT and let us know how it performs. It will be a good yardstick to let us know where your engine is, more or less.<P> Hint: find an open flat streach of road a half mile long, and drive down it first, then turn around and make the run going the other way. 0 - 60 full throttle is not really breaking any laws, but a cop in bad mood will think of something (drag racing your stopwatch?)||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||02:07 PM||||oh geeze, i just realized nobody asked the most obvious question:<P>what exactly did you loose at? 1/4 mile, redlight to redlight? best mpg rating?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>also, since you have a 5 speed, how high do you rev the engine before you upshift? 2,000 3,000 4,000...  <P>are you sure your tach is working correctly and you are not shifting too soon? what does it read in 5th gear at 65 mph?||||reg||1
Z||000019||ferrari_momo||05-15-2001||02:40 PM||||when i get me a job, i will put a set of ford injectors or 350 chevy injectors.  someone in the forum said that it will work good or very good.  what y'all think?<BR>pat<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Alex4mula||05-15-2001||02:41 PM||||3.4 injectors with stock PROM may work OK on closed loop mode where the O2 can control the amount of gas. But on WOT the ECM uses a table (no feedback) and can open to much the bigger 3.4 injector (rich) and hurt performance.<BR>We are talking here about the 3.4 pushrod engine with Fiero intake. No need for Camaro computer there.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Greg Piet||05-15-2001||04:56 PM||||I think the problem is as follows...<BR>Take the key, and put it in the ignition... then turn the key.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||88formula||05-15-2001||05:15 PM||||Everyone here is just guessing. 0-60 times are not going tell you that there is a problem with fuel. What you need to do is get a scan tool and look at the BLM and Integrator and see if they are lean or rich. If they are way off and everything is in working order than you need to have the prom chip recalibrated for your motor. <P>Bigger injectors are not needed especially for a stock 3.4 engine. The 2.8-liter injectors can flow enough fuel to flood that motor. You should however have them blueprinted. I had mine done here <A HREF="http://www.injector.com" TARGET=_blank>www.injector.com</A> <P>Using Dragstrip2000 a stock 3.4-liter engine in a fiero is good for 0-60 mph in 6.63 seconds and it should run the quarter mile in a t least 14.8 seconds @ 91.1 mph. That is with the five speed Getrag transmission and a 2925 lb. weight including the driver. It is very possible that those two cars are faster than your car. I have seen a stock looking Honda Civic do a 13.1 next to a C5 Corvette that did a 13.5 in the quarter mile at the track. The Honda spun out from the start too and had a terrible 60-foot time.   <P>You may have gotten beet by some form of forced induction like nitrous or a turbo. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||11:33 PM||||what does Dragstrip 2000 predict for a stock aircooled VW beetle, we're talking about a 2000 lb, 55HP car here that he lost to (the other one) that has a top speed of 88mph!||||reg||1
Z||000024||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||01:10 AM||||Ok Ferrari_Momo I've seen that you have quite a few Fieros and parts and kit cars etc. for sale so I would imagine you know how fast a stock 2.8 feels. Is you car faster than that? or slower?<P>There is no way that even an Iron Duke would loose to a stock Beetle much less a 3.4 unless it is running really terrible. <P>My guess is that the owner just told you it was "stock". I've seen some pretty fast beetles that are not stock but look it.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Alex4mula||05-16-2001||08:30 AM||||Pardon me 88formula but I'm not gessing. My 2.8 injectors are blueprinted and I know the don't lean becasue of many hrs testing and reading in my AutoXray scanner (BLM, O2, pulse, etc.). Otherwise I would not post here. Also a rich engine could function very bad. I have droped 1.5 sec on 0-60 just by lowering the fuel preasure which was sky high from the beginning (like 52PSI).||||reg||1
Z||000026||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||09:18 AM||||personally I think a standard performance test you can run on your car, like 0 to 60, is a great way to get a handle on what is going on. I ran mine again last night, and this time I didnt woose out on the pedal (I rocked my car pretty hard in park and the engine seems to be firmly attached)<P>  and this time I ran 0 to 60 in 8.7S. Since a new stock 2.8L will run it in around 8.4, and since I have an auto this tells me lots of things:<P>  I doubt that my cat is plugged, or that my exhaust is blocked<P>  I must be running on all 6 cylinders, the timing must be more or less correct, and the fuel mixture must be close.<P>   I see lots of posts on this forum where people say stuff like, "my car seems to run better when its damp out, or cold, or with the AC on, or when the stereo is playing heavy metal tunes at 120db....<P>  well, how much better?! feelings are subjective, 0 to 60 is objective. If the guy that started this thread can run 0 to 60 in 6 to 7 seconds, then his engine is not too far off, maybe not at all, maybe that stock bug had a turbo or N/O injection<P>  but on the other hand, if he runs in 11 S then either his engine is messed up, or he is lugging the shifts...  Its tough to diagnose someones complaints via email, you have to make a lot of assumptions.<P>  But, whenever something is not right, it always means that something you have assumed is correct, is actually not correct, and most of the time you look at the things that are really OK, and one of the things that you have assumed is ok, is the one thats screwing everything up.<P>  Some of the questions I am asking may sound stupid, but we are already past ego. The guys 3.4 Fiero got beat by a stock VW beetle, and he doesnt understand why. So ego is already gone, he's asking for help, which is a bold admission that he doesnt know what the problem is.<P>  this is a really interesting problem he has, and I am looking forward to seeing what the final outcome will be. <P>  I just realized I have assumed Pat is a guy, but looking at Pat's user name '- momo' maybe its Patrica? I always assume gearheads are guys, sorry if I got it backwards. Either way, your 3.4 has no gender recongnition detector, so it should run the same whether you are M or F.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||erikred||05-16-2001||11:45 AM||||Well if we do not know what type of race it was, we cannot speculate on what is wrong. If it was a short race like 1/8 mile or light to light, it is not suprising at all that a Honda or VW could beat him. I have a 350 HP TA that runs between 13-12 sec. in the quarter. I got smoked by a 80s model Cutlass Cierra beater with a 2.5. I know for sure it was stock because I knew the driver. My father owned a 325 HP Camaro SS 396 in the early 70s. He was consistantly beaten by his buddy in a stock 6cyl Chevy II when they had a hole shot contest. <P>Those Hondas are hard to beat on a short track. If you want to beat them the only way is to go lighter. On the street no amount of power is going to make up for a significantly heavier car.||||reg||10
Z||000028||LottaBallsCamaro||05-16-2001||12:54 PM||||What kind of stickers did the civic have? if it had a lot of racing stickers then that civic could definatly beat you!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000029||87ECO5||05-16-2001||01:05 PM||||Don't know how signifigant this is but ...<P>My wife and I dragraced last weekend; her in her 1992 Sunbird convertible with 2.0 4 cylinder and 3-speed automatic, and me in my '87 coupe with remanufactured 3.4 and Isuzu 5-speed.<P>I had a tough time pulling away (of course, I told her I was just playing with her).<P>I'm running with the stock 2.8 injectors and ECM, but my buddies like to tell me (over and over and over) that I should upgrade to the larger injectors and FPR.||||reg||1
Z||000030||ferrari_momo||05-16-2001||08:53 PM||||honestly, i donot know how 2.8 runs. mine ran poorly so i did the 3.4 deal. one of a fiero member in OK took me in his 2.8, and it was pretty quick.  i think i will go with turbo. if not fast enough, i may add nitro. do they make nitro for 3.4 fieros?  at this point, i do not care if i blow up this motor.  got a v8 fiero, been sitting for 10 years now.  has less than 500 miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  speed is not on my side.  <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Slammed Fiero||05-16-2001||10:27 PM||||I think you need to sort out your car before you get a Turbo for it. My 86 GT 2.8 with minimal mods will turn consistent high 6's to 60. <P>Whats done to the motor<BR>Fuel press regulator?<BR>Injector size<BR>Chip you are using??<BR>what was swapped over?<BR>Is the Cat clogged<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000032||FieroLogun||05-16-2001||11:00 PM|||| If you go with a Northstar/Twinturbo git the Heavy duty axles, I didnt git them and a day later they were broke <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> With 450hp on tap you need high quality parts.<P> If you go with a stock Northstar 300hp the stock axels will work fine.<P>------------------<BR>88 GT Twin Turbo Northstar 450hp 12.02 1/4<BR>4.1 0-60 (bad traction:)||||reg||1
Z||000033||ferrari_momo||05-16-2001||11:15 PM||||has anyone ever put nitrous system in thier fieros? need more info.  thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||11:48 PM||||fierri momo,<P>  so far you havent answered any of the questions we have been asking you. <P>If you cant get the fuel injected 3.4 running well enough to go 0 to 60 in 8 seconds, putting N/O or a turbo on it is only gonna make it worse, not better.<P>you need to start thinking "finely tuned" instead of 'brute force'. Throwing money and add ons at your car is not gonna make it faster.<P>Im not trying to be insulting here, it sounds like you could not get your 2.8 V6 to run right, now you cant get the 3.4 to run right, you either gotta get your head into this thing and decide you are going to DO it and get it right<P> or take it to someone who can<P> or stop throwing money at your Fieros and go out and buy a fast car from a dealer.<P>  A fuel injected internal combustion engine is a complex machine - there is a very narrow window within which it will run well. It may seem frustrating, but when you finally get the engine right, it will be all the more sweet, cause you did it yourself and you will understand whats going on under that hood.<P>  Think of it this way. All those hoses and wires and sensors and pumps and circuit boards are in there for a reason. Each one has a task and a purpose, otherwise the engineers in detriot would not have spent the money to put them in there.<P>  All it takes is for one of those things under the hood to screw up and the car wont run right. You may have to go through the drive train one step at a time to get everything right, but thats what car restoration and modification is all about.<P>  Hang in there, stick it out, get the 3.4 running well and it will lite up your tires and push you back in that bucket seat like you wont believe.<P>  Then if you still want to go faster start thinking about N/O or turbos.||||reg||1
Z||000035||ferrari_momo||05-17-2001||12:18 AM||||thanks Ken,<BR>  well, i didnt do the conversion myself.  i am goin to redo the work myself now.  will let you all know.  yeah, i think something is not plugged up or something.  may be vacuum?  perhaps those 3.4 injectors that causes the headaches.  the car stil uses 2.8 computer. but it doesn't make sense, if injectors does matter.  becoz i hear people put 350 chevy or ford injectors in 3.4 motor.  do you know anything about it?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||12:38 AM||||Im not an expert on putting other engines into fieros, but one though just occurred to me.<P> You say the car was a 2.8 V6, and it wasnt running right, and you had the 3.4L put in to fix it.<P> Its possible you re-used the thing that was messing up the 2.8 on the 3.4? did the car ever run well after you had the 3.4 put in?<P> did the place that swapped the engine offer any followup maintainace, warrenty, or help with problems?||||reg||1
Z||000037||ferrari_momo||05-17-2001||08:46 AM||||well,<BR> the mechanic looked at the 2.8 i had.  he fixed the problem.  it was off timing.  it ran fine afterwards.  but i had the 3.4 installed anyways.  i was hoping to get more out of 3.4 engine.  he also mentioned that 3.4 injectors is the problem.  but i do not get it, if 3.4 injectors made such effects on the car.  how could other people use ford or chevy 350 injectors?  i am goin to look more into it.  all of my sensors are new.  i am not sure what could be wrong.  there is no ECM code appear when i checked.  must be some stupid wiring ot vacuum line leak.  will keep you all updated.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000038||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||09:34 AM||||Injectors are designed for different cars with really only one important variation, then the valve is open it allows a preset amount of fuel to flow through.<P>  Like a nozzel on a garden hose. One injector with 40psi of fuel pressure might let an ounce of fuel through in 5 seconds (if held on) while another might let 2 ounces through in 5 seconds (im just making these numbers up for an example - I dont know that the acutal rates are).<P>  So each injector will have a flow rate spec, and you can probabally find them if you know where to look. So its very possible the injectors from  a ford engine might have the flow rate you need, and the injectors you have on there now may be way off.<P>  The guy that put the engine in the car is your best resource right now. Unless you have decided the guy is a total bonehead, then take his advice and let him either put the injectors he thinks it needs in there, or get the info from him and you put them in.<P>  Im willing to bet a set of new injectors would be expensive, or having him put them in might be costly? Or maybe he overlooked the injectors when he did the swap and now you question his judgment?<P>  If your not sure what injectors your engine needs - I'd start a new thread: "Proper injectors for GM 3.4L engine are ?" and ask for answers from people that have already made the swap.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Gumball||05-18-2001||12:00 AM||||Was your E-brake on.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
